ll entries and invocation), and `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` (lines 1650–2390).\nI will now check the namespace imports of `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` to locate `yang.call` and read the PHP call/closure entry definitions in `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc`.\nI will now read `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` and the earlier sections of `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`.\nI will now read `src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc` and `src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc`.\nI will now inspect `test/yang/clojure_test.clj` to examine the outcome model tests, constant-depth tail recursion assertions, `yin.k/outcome-of`, and LaTTe/crosshost verification dependencies.\nI will now read the remainder of `test/yang/clojure_test.clj` and check `docs/design/yang.clojure.crosshost.md` (if present) for the slice-5 and A1–A5 architecture rulings.\nI will now read the relevant design documents under `docs/design/` to locate the slice 5 and A1–A5 rulings.\nI will read the header of `test/yang/clojure_test.clj` and `src/dev/subsystems.edn` to see the related test and design references.\nI will now check the LaTTe and crosshost test/design files to see how they depend on `yang.clojure` and the outcome model.\nI will now read lines 600–800 of `test/yang/clojure_test.clj` and `test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj`.\nI will now read `test/yang/call/conformance.clj` to see how cross-language composition and exception boundaries are tested across the four frontends.\nI will now read the remainder of `test/yang/call/conformance.clj` and lines 3650–3838 of `docs/design/yang.antlr.md`.\nI will check for the LaTTe / cross-host test files and `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` to verify the exact file and line citations for slice 5, A1–A5, and LaTTe/crosshost verification.\nI will now read lines 201–500 and 3100–3419 of `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, and check `test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj` and `test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj`.\nI will read `collab/exception-track/1794332020000-architect-value-path-refusal-shape.prompt.md` to see the context of the prior adjudication referenced in the prompt.\nI will read the findings file for `1794332020000-architect-value-path-refusal-shape`.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-10 19:27:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 02:27:00 +07 (+0700)\n\n## Ruling: **(A) Keep v2 as-is** (Boundary uniformity at every admitted guest-call boundary; `yang.clojure` keeps its internal outcome encoding subject to R4's equivalence proof, and `yang.python` / `yang.typescript` / `yang.php` keep their continuation-handler preludes)\n\n**Dominant tradeoff:** Unifying on `%capture` continuations **(B)** destroys `yang.clojure`'s constant-depth tail recursion and cell-free closure purity (invalidating slice 5 and the LaTTe kernel gate), while unifying on outcome tuples **(C)** imposes a universal completion-packet allocation and branch tax on every subexpression across 14,100+ lines of `py`/`ts`/`php` preludes that still require `%capture` for `finally`, `return`/`break`/`continue`, `with`, and destructors — whereas keeping **v2 boundary uniformity (A)** pays neither cost because the `%capture` bridge at [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1506-1629`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1506-L1629) and [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1283-1356`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1283-L1356) already translates between the two encodings at the exact `yang.call` boundary.\n\n*(Authority note: [Ruling of Record v2](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md#L1-L31) is already an owner-approved decision (`2026-11-07`) that explicitly separated boundary uniformity from internal lowering at line `:30` and `:69-75`. Upholding **(A)** requires no new owner product call; overturning v2 to adopt **(B)** or **(C)** would reopen an owner ruling and the closed LaTTe/crosshost gates.)*\n\n---\n\n### 1. Evidence Against **(B)** (Porting `yang.clojure` to `%capture` Continuation Handlers)\n\n1. **Why `%capture` handler stacks break constant-depth tail calls on `:semantic`, `:stack`, and `:register`:**\n   - In `yin.vm`, `:application` nodes carry a `:tail?` boolean ([`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1005, 1026`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc#L1005)). On the register/semantic VM, a `:call` instruction pushes a `:return` frame onto `K` whenever `tail?` is `false` ([`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:194-199`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc#L194-L199)).\n   - `yang.tails/mark-tails` ([`src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc:11-36`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc#L11-L36)) marks `:tail? true` only on applications in the tail position of a `:lambda` body (threading through `:if` branches at `:21-25`), and walks all application operands with `tail? = false` (`:30-31`).\n   - In the continuation-handler encoding used by Python ([`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:315-328, 906-914`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L315-L328)), TypeScript ([`src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1725-1735`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1725-L1735)), and PHP ([`src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:258-271`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L258-L271)), every `try` block and every bridged `function-entry` must save the mutable context cell (`*.rt/ctx`), push a `:handler` frame before calling `(body)`, and **restore the saved context after `(body)` returns normally**:\n     ```clojure\n     ;; src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1732-1733\n     (js/set-handlers! (js/frame :handler r (get saved :handlers)))\n     (let [v (body)] (do (js/restore! saved) v))\n     ```\n   - Because `u/let1` / `u/then` lowers `(let [v (body)] (do (restore! saved) v))` to `((fn [v] ...) (body))` ([`src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:65-79`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc#L65-L79)), `(body)` is an **operand** of an application (`tail? = false`). Every activation that installs and restores a dynamic handler frame on `*.rt/ctx` therefore pushes a CESK continuation frame onto `K`.\n   - By contrast, under `yang.clojure`'s slice-5 outcome model ([`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:838-868`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L838-L868)), there is no mutable handler stack to restore after a call. Consequently, in `call-entry` ([`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1283-1356`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1283-L1356)), both the static intra-unit token-packet lane (`(u/app (u/v body) (u/app (u/v 'data/nth) (u/v invocation) (u/lit 1)))` at `:1336`, invoked via `dsg__intra_call` at `:1711-1717, :2554-2568`) and the dynamic tail `:clj/outcome-token` lane (`(u/app (u/v body) positional)` at `:1352`, invoked via `compile-guest-call` at `:1563-1572`) invoke `body` in **true tail position** (`:tail? true`).\n   - This is directly gated by `test-outcome-common-call-depth` ([`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1584-1618`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj#L1584-L1618)), which proves on both `:walker` and `:semantic` that direct self-tail calls (`cnt -\u003e cnt`) and 3-way mutual tail recursion (`a -\u003e b -\u003e c -\u003e a`) execute 1,000 iterations at constant continuation depth (`(= d100 d1000)` and `(\u003c= d1000 (+ d0 2))` at `:1611-1614`).\n\n2. **Cell-free closure purity, instruction budgets, and LaTTe kernel verification:**\n   - Every `%capture`-based `try` allocates a mutable cell `(cell/new :first)` per `try` activation and mutates a task-global cell (`*.rt/ctx`) via `cell/set!` ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:317-327`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L317-L327)). In `yang.clojure`, function descriptors are pure immutable maps ([`compile-call-descriptor`, `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1378-1386`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1378-L1386)), and `try`/`catch` lowers to pure lexical `dsg__let` + `if` over immutable `[:yin.k/ok v]` / `[:yin.k/error d]` vectors ([`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1126-1143`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1126-L1143)), keeping guest closures free of cell references for UCF machine-data validation and lift ([`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2584-2585`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2584-L2585), [`docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3833-3835`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md#L3833-L3835)).\n   - `yang.clojure`'s guest library ([`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2672-2680, 2820-2845`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2672-L2680)) derives `string?`, `coll?`, `vector?`, `map?`, and `set?` from `outcome-adapter` ([`:1211-1228`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1211-L1228)) and `(yin.k/outcome-of ...)` ([`:997-1002, :2165-2166`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L997-L1002)).\n   - Both the tight intra-unit call step budget (`160,046` walker steps / `86,822` semantic steps for 1,000 calls at [`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2602-2623`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj#L2602-L2623)) and the closed 54-fixture LaTTe kernel differential gate ([`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:179-184, 239-270, 3006-3385`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L3006-L3385)) depend directly on the outcome model. Reopening them is costly and unnecessary.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Evidence Against **(C)** (Porting `yang.python`, `yang.typescript`, `yang.php` to Outcome Tuples)\n\n1. **Violates §8.10.7's normal-return invariant:**\n   - [`docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3840-3842`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md#L3840-L3842) states: *\"Normal return is the value of ordinary Yin application. There is no extra return continuation, completion packet around each result, or new language-specific return instruction.\"* Turning every function and helper return across all frontends into `[:yin.k/ok value]` turns a Clojure-internal pass into a universal completion-packet wrapper.\n2. **`py`, `ts`, and `php` still require `%capture` and dynamic frame stacks for non-exception control flow:**\n   - Unlike `yang.clojure` (which has no `finally`, no `return`/`break`/`continue`, and no scope-exit destructors; [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1131-1137`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1131-L1137)), the three ANTLR frontends use the exact same `%capture` + `:finally` frame stack (`py/unwind-to` at [`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:304-314`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L304-L314), `js/unwind-to` at [`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1712-1724`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1712-L1724), `php/unwind-to` at [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:220-230`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L220-L230)) to unwind non-local `return`, `break`, and `continue` through `try ... finally` (`py/call-ec` at `:339-351`, `js/call-ec` at `:1744-1756`, `php/call-ec` at `:282-296`), Python `with` managers (`py/with-bound` at `:369-395`), PHP terminal `exit`/fatal escapes (`php/abandon-to` / `php/call-terminal` at [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:231-257`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L231-L257)), and PHP deterministic `__destruct` finalization (`php/pending-add!` / `php/flush-pending!` at [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4178-4206`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L4178-L4206)).\n   - Because almost every operation in Python, JS/TS, and PHP (property reads, operator dunders, indexing, type coercions) can raise, porting them to outcome tuples would require rewriting \u003e14,100 lines of preludes ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc) 3,781 lines, [`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc) 4,298 lines, [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc) 6,025 lines) and their lowerers to allocate and branch on 2-element vectors after every subexpression, while still keeping `%capture` for `call-ec` and generators.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why Factoring the `py`/`js`/`php` `try`-Family Into a Shared Prelude Is NOT Justified Now (Rejecting **(D)**)\n\nAlthough `py/try`, `js/try`, and `php/try` share the same structural pattern over `%capture`, inspecting the three implementations shows five concrete semantic differences across ~75 lines of code:\n1. **Context cell schema \u0026 `restore!` semantics:** `js.rt/ctx` holds only `{:handlers hs}` and `js/restore!` overwrites the whole map ([`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1690-1695`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1690-L1695)), whereas `py.rt/ctx` and `php.rt/ctx` hold `{:handlers :depth :base :frame}` and their `restore!` preserves `:base` across unwinds for recursion-depth accounting ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:260-278`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L260-L278), [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:178-180`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L178-L180)).\n2. **Frame tuple width \u0026 `pop-frame`:** `js/frame` is a 4-tuple `[tag payload rest depth-count]` ([`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1697-1703`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1697-L1703)), whereas `py/frame` and `php/frame` are 5-tuples that also snapshot and restore call `:depth` at index 4 ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:283-293`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L283-L293), [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:185-195`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L185-L195)).\n3. **`try` arity \u0026 normal-exit value:** `js/try` is binary `(fn [body handler] ...)` returning `(body)`'s value ([`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1725-1735`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1725-L1735)), whereas `py/try` and `php/try` are ternary `(fn [body handler orelse] ...)` discarding `(body)` and tail-calling `(orelse)` after `(restore! saved)` ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:315-328`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L315-L328), [`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:258-271`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L258-L271)).\n4. **Empty-stack sentinel vs defect trap:** `py/raise` and `js/throw` return `(:py/no-handler e)` / `(:js/no-handler e)` on an empty handler stack ([`python/antlr/prelude.cljc:298`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc#L298), [`typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1708`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc#L1708)), whereas `php/raise` invokes `(php/invariant-violation \"php/raise: empty handler stack\")` ([`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:196-215`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L196-L215)).\n5. **Language-specific unwinds \u0026 unit-scoping:** PHP has `php/abandon-to` / `php/call-terminal` skipping `:finally` ([`php/antlr/prelude.cljc:231-257`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc#L231-L257)), each prelude binds its own module-internal store key (`py.rt/ctx`, `js.rt/ctx`, `php.rt/ctx`) via `yang.core.prelude/module-definitions` ([`src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc:44-50`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc#L44-L50)), and touching `yang.python.antlr.prelude`'s AST structure forces C4 scanner golden regeneration ([`docs/agents/known-gaps.md:40`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/known-gaps.md#L40)). None of the R1–R5 defects in Ruling v2 are in those ~75 lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Concrete Follow-Up Work Implied by Ruling **(A)**\n\n1. **Follow Ruling v2's sequencing without scope expansion ([`collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md:118-132`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md#L118-L132)):**\n   - **Round 1 (JS R1 + R3):** Install the `:yang.call/raise` bridge before argument binding in `js/make-function-nt` (`src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581-1596`) and retain/forward full protocol conditions in `js/call` (`:1627-1638`).\n   - **Round 2 (PHP R1 + R2 + R3):** Install the raise bridge on PHP function and Closure entries and repair reception/rethrow with `ForeignException` condition retention (`src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2250-2410`).\n   - **Round 3 (Python R2 + R3):** Add `ForeignException` reception and condition-preserving egress in `py/function-entry` (`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:911-913`) and `py/call-kw` (`:1043-1046`).\n   - **Round 4a (Clojure R3):** Complete the `dsg__call_token`-authenticated `:dsg__condition` provenance tag and `remark-tails` fix in `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` per adjudications `1794320926000` and `1794332020000`.\n2. **Round 4b (Clojure R4 — Private-vs-Public Equivalence Proof \u0026 Guard):**\n   - Execute the pending ledger obligation `:clojure-private-public-equivalence` ([`test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj:175-177`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj#L175-L177)) by testing all **three** Clojure call lanes in [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc) against identical scenarios (`:caught`, `:uncaught`, `:protocol-rethrow`, `:binding`, `:higher-order`):\n     1. Static intra-unit packet call (`dsg__intra_call` -\u003e `call-entry` vector branch, [`clojure.cljc:1324-1336, 1711-1717, 2554-2568`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1324-L1336)),\n     2. Dynamic tail call (`compile-guest-call` `:clj/outcome-token` branch -\u003e `call-entry` token branch, [`clojure.cljc:1346-1353, 1563-1572`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1563-L1572)), and\n     3. Dynamic non-tail / public boundary call (`compile-guest-call` `public-bridge` via `u/capture` -\u003e `call-entry` `entry-body`, [`clojure.cljc:1300-1312, 1518-1562`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1518-L1562)).\n   - **Specific R4 audit point to verify in Round 4b:** In [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1565`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1565), `compile-guest-call` branches on `(dsg__get callee :clj/outcomes?)` (a plain boolean set at `:1385`) before sending `{:clj/outcome-token dsg__call_token}` to `call-entry` (`:1346-1353`). Within a single unit this is guarded because `dsg__admitted?` (`:2534-2548`) only admits non-cell-ref maps whose `:clj/admission` witness matches the caller unit's `dsg__call_token`. However, if a Clojure callable from a *different* compiled unit (whose `dsg__call_token` has a different `:segment` on `:semantic`/`:stack`/`:register`, [`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:164-169`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc#L164-L169)) is ever invoked across units (e.g. `clojure -\u003e python -\u003e clojure` A→B→A or `clojure -\u003e clojure`), `dsg__admitted?` and `:clj/outcomes?` must not disagree with `call-entry`'s `dsg__call_token` check. Round 4b must include an explicit cross-unit Clojure callable test (`clojure -\u003e clojure` and `clojure -\u003e python -\u003e clo